::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 3<sup>2</sup> &middot; 3<sup>3</sup>. {
~A. 27#This adds the exponents correctly but stops at 3<sup>3</sup>. Product of powers gives 3<sup>5</sup>, then evaluate.
=B. 243#Correct. 3<sup>2</sup> &middot; 3<sup>3</sup> = 3<sup>5</sup> = 243.
~C. 729#This multiplies the exponents to get 3<sup>6</sup>. When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents.
~D. 18#This multiplies the visible numbers instead of using exponent properties.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite x<sup>4</sup> &middot; x<sup>5</sup>. {
~A. x<sup>20</sup>#This multiplies the exponents. Product of powers uses addition of exponents.
~B. 2x<sup>9</sup>#This adds an extra coefficient. The expression only combines powers of x.
~C. x#This subtracts exponents instead of adding them. Subtraction is used for quotients, not products.
=D. x<sup>9</sup>#Correct. The bases match, so add the exponents: 4 + 5 = 9.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite (a<sup>3</sup>)<sup>2</sup>. {
=A. a<sup>6</sup>#Correct. A power raised to a power uses multiplication of exponents: 3 &middot; 2 = 6.
~B. a<sup>5</sup>#This adds the exponents. For a power raised to a power, multiply the exponents.
~C. a<sup>9</sup>#This squares the exponent 3. The outside exponent means multiply 3 by 2.
~D. 2a<sup>3</sup>#This treats the outside exponent like a coefficient. Exponents do not become coefficients.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 64<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup>. {
~A. 32#This divides 64 by 2. An exponent of <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> means square root, not divide by 2.
~B. 4#This is &radic;16, not &radic;64. Use the square root of 64.
=C. 8#Correct. 64<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup> = &radic;64 = 8.
~D. 128#This multiplies by 2. A rational exponent of <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> represents a root.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite z<sup>-3</sup> with a positive exponent. {
~A. z<sup>3</sup>#This changes the sign of the exponent without moving the factor. A negative exponent moves the factor to the denominator.
=B. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>z<sup>3</sup></sub>#Correct. z<sup>-3</sup> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>z<sup>3</sup></sub>.
~C. -z<sup>3</sup>#A negative exponent does not make the expression negative. It creates a reciprocal.
~D. <sup>z</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>#This treats the exponent like division by 3. Use the reciprocal with z<sup>3</sup> in the denominator.




}
